course: dictionary
lesson: 13601
title: Incorporation: joining things into a whole
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Incorporation comes from the verb incorporate, which means to take something in and make it part of a bigger whole. Its word parts help: in means into, and corpus means body. So incorporation is putting things into one body.
explain: «The school discussed the incorporation of new ideas into the science course.» The new ideas become part of the bigger course. Ask yourself: what is being joined to what? Here, the ideas are joined to the course.
explain: Grown-ups also use incorporation for businesses and towns. «The shop completed its incorporation as a company last year» means it became an official company. «The incorporation of the village into the city brought new buses» means a village joined a larger town.
fact: Incorporation means including something as part of a bigger whole.
fact: In business and law, incorporation means making a business into an official company, called a corporation.
fact: Incorporation can also mean joining a town or area into another one.
fact: Articles of incorporation are the official papers that set up a company.
fact: In the USA, you may see «Inc.» after a company name: it is short for incorporated.
exercise: «The incorporation of new ideas made the plan better.» What does incorporation mean here? | *Including new ideas as part of the plan | Shrinking the plan to fit in a box | Throwing the plan away | Printing the plan on paper
exercise: «The town's incorporation into the county happened in 1950.» What does incorporation mean here? | Building a new school in the town | Losing all the town's people | Painting the town hall | *Joining the town into a larger area
exercise: What is a business called after its incorporation, when it becomes an official company? | A garden | Mud | *A corporation | A puzzle
test: What does the word incorporation mean? | *Including something as part of a bigger whole | Painting something bright | Leaving something out completely | Taking something apart into small bits
test: In business, what does incorporation mean? | Closing the business for ever | *Making the business into an official company | Moving the business to the sea | Giving the business away
exam: Where would you most likely find «articles of incorporation»? | Recipes for cakes | Comic strips about animals | *Formal papers about setting up a company | Poems written for fun
